<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Love These Entertainers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of the main plot and characters in short stories. Reading: Summarize sections of texts in written form. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by reading base words and inflectional endings (-s, -es, -ed, -ing).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of the plot in a short story (e.g., "This is Adalberto Carrasquilla. He is one of the best soccer players in Panama.")<br>Can summarize and extract key details from a text about the achievements of famous people (e.g., "She is Atheyna Bylon. She is a famous boxer and an Olympic medalist.")<br>Can read sentences fluently by recognizing and pronouncing base words with inflectional endings (-ed, -ing), demonstrating phonemic awareness (e.g., "He improved his own marks.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a few pictures of famous Panamanian athletes and entertainers (e.g., Mariano Rivera, Sech, Atheyna Bylon). Students are asked to identify them and recall one thing they know about them. "Who is this person? What is their job?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a picture, for example, of Roberto Durán. "This is Roberto Durán. He is a famous athlete, a boxer. He achieved many awards. He is considered one of the greatest boxers in the world." The teacher models pronunciation of 'famous,' 'athlete,' 'achieved,' and 'greatest.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is Roberto Durán a singer or a boxer?" (Expected answer: A boxer.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'famous' mean? Is he known by many people?" (Expected answer: Yes, many people know him.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a handout with short biographical texts (2-3 paragraphs each) about 2-3 different famous Panamanians (e.g., an athlete, a singer, an actor). Each text includes pictures. The texts integrate target vocabulary (entertainer, athlete, achievement, award, singer, actor, recognition, competition, performance, sports fan; win, achieve, inspire, train, perform, compete; famous, popular, talented, skilled, specialized; more, best) and grammar (present continuous for future events, comparatives and superlatives, past simple for achievements).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example text excerpt:</i> "Atheyna Bylon is a famous boxer and an Olympic medalist. She won a medal in the Olympic Games. Atheyna is training for a new tournament this year. She is more talented than some other boxers."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading short, simple texts on familiar topics and extracting key information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> The teacher asks, "What are these texts generally about?" (Expected answer: Famous Panamanians and their achievements.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read 3-4 True/False statements about the texts and mark their answers (e.g., "Atheyna Bylon is a famous singer. (False)").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match the name of each famous person to their profession (e.g., Roberto Durán – Boxer, Sech – Singer).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying specific details, recognizing and using comparative/superlative adjectives, past simple verbs for achievements, and phonemic awareness of -ed and -ing endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Cloze/Gap-fill):</b> Students receive sentences with blanks from the biographical texts. They must complete the sentences using target vocabulary words provided in a word bank (e.g., "Mariano Rivera ____ many awards." [won]). This reinforces vocabulary introduced in Lesson 1 within the reading context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice (Grammar & Phonemic Awareness):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read sentences from the text aloud in pairs, focusing on base words and inflectional endings. They underline all verbs with '-ed' and '-ing' endings (e.g., "She <u>won</u> a medal," "Atheyna is <u>training</u> for a new tournament," "He <u>improved</u> his own marks."). The teacher models correct pronunciation for a few examples like "performed" /pərˈfɔːrmd/ and "watching" /ˈwɒtʃɪŋ/.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then answer questions or complete sentences using comparatives or superlatives based on the texts (e.g., "Roberto Durán is ____ (good) known than other boxers." -> "Roberto Durán is *better* known than other boxers.").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair interaction (Peer Check & Detail Extraction):</b> In pairs, students formulate and ask each other 3-4 "Wh-" questions about the texts (e.g., "Who is Atheyna Bylon?", "What did Mariano Rivera achieve?"). They then take turns reading a chosen sentence containing an -ed or -ing ending to their partner, who confirms correct pronunciation.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task:</b> "Achievement Summary Report"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students individually choose one famous Panamanian from the texts. Their task is to write a short summary (3-4 sentences) about that person's key achievements, using the past simple tense and at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., 'achieved,' 'won,' 'award,' 'talented'). They also need to include one comparative or superlative sentence. After writing, students read their summaries aloud to a partner, focusing on fluency and clear pronunciation, especially of -ed/-ing endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example:</i> "This is Adalberto Carrasquilla. He is one of the best soccer players in Panama. He played for many teams and won important matches. He improved his own marks in several competitions. He is more popular than some other players in the league."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment and self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the 'Achievement Summary Report' and review of student written summaries.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short worksheet.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Match the Achievement:</b> Match each famous person to one of their significant achievements mentioned in the text (e.g., "Mariano Rivera" -> "Won many awards as a baseball player.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarize & Identify:</b> Answer 2-3 short comprehension questions about specific details in the text (e.g., "What is Atheyna Bylon doing for a new tournament?"). Identify and write down two words from the text with '-ed' endings and two words with '-ing' endings.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows comprehension of main plot and characters in short stories (answers "Match the Achievement" correctly).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes sections of texts in written form (answers comprehension questions accurately).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying base words and inflectional endings (-ed, -ing).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:<br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can show comprehension of a story about a famous Panamanian by..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read sentences with '-ed' and '-ing' words like..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting achievement you read about today and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which part of reading about famous people was easy for you, and which part was more challenging?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., Student engagement with the biographies, effective identification of -ed/-ing words.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially to reinforce summarization skills and fluent reading of inflectional endings?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson provided information about famous Panamanians and their achievements. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use this information to discuss and compare these figures, sharing what they have read with their peers in active conversations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>